1
00:00:03,754 --> 00:00:04,754
Let's go. Let's go.

2
00:00:04,839 --> 00:00:05,923
We're gonna be late!

3
00:00:06,340 --> 00:00:10,344
Fun fact... the first time I almost died was at the age of nine.

4
00:00:10,553 --> 00:00:11,553
The murder weapon?

5
00:00:11,596 --> 00:00:13,264
A Jimmy Dean sausage.

6
00:00:18,644 --> 00:00:21,355
Mom, Sheldon's making faces at me.

7
00:00:23,733 --> 00:00:24,358
Sheldon!

8
00:00:24,567 --> 00:00:25,752
In fairness to Mr. Dean...

9
00:00:25,776 --> 00:00:32,992
Honey! This one morning, I decided to forgo my normal 20 chews per bite, as prescribed by the American medical association.

10
00:00:33,201 --> 00:00:34,201
George, help!

11
00:00:36,496 --> 00:00:39,081
Come here, boy. We got it. We just got to smack it out.

12
00:00:39,290 --> 00:00:45,880
To further complicate matters, in any real-life crisis, my family's default mode is mindless panic.

13
00:00:46,088 --> 00:00:48,132
Sheldon's gonna die! Sheldon's gonna die!

14
00:00:48,341 --> 00:00:49,717
Or heartless apathy.

15
00:00:50,259 --> 00:00:51,636
Oh, lord.

16
00:00:51,844 --> 00:00:53,164
Here we go. This is how you do it.

17
00:00:53,346 --> 00:00:55,640
Here we go. And we shake. Honey!

18
00:00:56,057 --> 00:01:00,061
It's interesting the things you think about when life is ebbing from your body.

19
00:01:00,269 --> 00:01:03,314
For instance, linoleum. What is it, really?

20
00:01:03,523 --> 00:01:06,651
Plastic? And if so, how is it different from formica?

21
00:01:06,859 --> 00:01:08,194
I'm calling 911.

22
00:01:10,488 --> 00:01:12,208
And what about count chocula?

23
00:01:12,448 --> 00:01:13,699
How is he a count?

24
00:01:13,908 --> 00:01:15,326
Did the title come with land?

25
00:01:15,535 --> 00:01:16,535
George, heimlich!

26
00:01:18,204 --> 00:01:20,540
Don't hurt his little ribs!

27
00:01:20,748 --> 00:01:24,794
They say, in the final moments, your life passes before your eyes.

28
00:01:25,044 --> 00:01:29,715
All I saw was my brother licking jelly off the knife and putting it back in the jar.

29
00:01:31,801 --> 00:01:33,219
Okay, it's better now. Sheldon!

30
00:01:33,469 --> 00:01:36,180
Honey. Are you okay?

31
00:01:36,389 --> 00:01:37,807
Can you breathe? Say something!

32
00:01:38,015 --> 00:01:40,810
You have to... throw away... that jelly.

33
00:01:44,021 --> 00:01:46,774
♪ nobody else is stronger than I am

34
00:01:46,983 --> 00:01:49,610
♪ yesterday I moved a mountain

35
00:01:49,819 --> 00:01:52,405
♪ I bet I could be your hero

36
00:01:52,613 --> 00:01:55,032
♪ I am a mighty little man

37
00:01:55,241 --> 00:01:58,536
♪ I am a mighty little man.

38
00:02:05,334 --> 00:02:07,503
I've never been late to school before.

39
00:02:07,712 --> 00:02:09,755
I'm late all the time. It's no big deal.

40
00:02:09,964 --> 00:02:12,592
I get you to school on time every day. Why are you late?

41
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:15,219
I have a lot of people to say hi to in the morning.

42
00:02:15,428 --> 00:02:17,462
Mom, would you write a note for me?

43
00:02:18,014 --> 00:02:18,806
Sure.

44
00:02:19,015 --> 00:02:20,725
You have the coolest excuse.

45
00:02:20,933 --> 00:02:23,519
You almost died. Did you see Jesus?

46
00:02:23,811 --> 00:02:26,022
I saw count chocula.

47
00:02:26,689 --> 00:02:29,692
But feel free to mention my brush with death in the note.

48
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:32,278
All right, that's enough talk about death and dying.

49
00:02:33,362 --> 00:02:36,198
I came this close to having my own room.

50
00:02:39,452 --> 00:02:41,871
Remember, the food drive ends Friday.

51
00:02:42,079 --> 00:02:43,748
All canned goods are welcome.

52
00:02:44,040 --> 00:02:47,835
But please... make sure your cans have labels.

53
00:02:48,044 --> 00:02:51,672
Needy people have enough problems without having to guess what's for dinner.

54
00:02:51,881 --> 00:02:54,425
I'm sorry I'm late. I have a note.

55
00:02:57,303 --> 00:03:00,014
You poor thing, you had a medical emergency?

56
00:03:00,222 --> 00:03:02,767
Yes, ma'am. I choked on a sausage.

57
00:03:04,018 --> 00:03:05,144
How big was it?

58
00:03:05,603 --> 00:03:07,271
About yay big.

59
00:03:11,025 --> 00:03:14,445
To this day, I still don't understand why they were laughing.

60
00:03:21,160 --> 00:03:22,703
You're sitting in my spot.

61
00:03:23,996 --> 00:03:24,996
Why is it your spot?

62
00:03:25,039 --> 00:03:26,749
It's complicated. Just move.

63
00:03:33,798 --> 00:03:36,884
You're reading a comic book. I am.

64
00:03:37,176 --> 00:03:39,595
You do understand those are for children.

65
00:03:40,262 --> 00:03:41,262
Have you ever read one?

66
00:03:41,639 --> 00:03:44,225
I outgrew picture books when I was three.

67
00:03:44,433 --> 00:03:45,433
Just eat your lunch.

68
00:04:02,535 --> 00:04:03,535
What's the matter?

69
00:04:04,662 --> 00:04:06,706
I-I don't think I can eat this.

70
00:04:06,914 --> 00:04:08,124
You want to trade?

71
00:04:08,749 --> 00:04:10,209
My mom made me fish soup again.

72
00:04:10,418 --> 00:04:13,546
How long do we have to be in this country before I get baloney?

73
00:04:17,007 --> 00:04:19,343
Mm, no, thank you.

74
00:04:19,552 --> 00:04:20,803
I'm not hungry.

75
00:04:21,595 --> 00:04:22,930
"I'm not hungry."

76
00:04:24,223 --> 00:04:26,308
Three words you never hear in Vietnam.

77
00:04:28,060 --> 00:04:29,979
Well, here's something nice.

78
00:04:30,187 --> 00:04:34,400
You know that Mr. Rosenbloom with the furniture store over by the steakhouse?

79
00:04:34,775 --> 00:04:37,027
I don't think so. Oh, sure you do.

80
00:04:37,236 --> 00:04:38,779
That Hebrew fella with the comb-over?

81
00:04:38,988 --> 00:04:40,030
You mean Jewish?

82
00:04:40,322 --> 00:04:42,491
I think they like to be called Hebrew.

83
00:04:43,284 --> 00:04:46,829
Anyway, he's asked me to dinner.

84
00:04:47,955 --> 00:04:49,790
Well, good for you. What'd you say?

85
00:04:50,166 --> 00:04:52,001
I said I'd have to think about it.

86
00:04:52,209 --> 00:04:53,209
What's to think about?

87
00:04:53,377 --> 00:04:54,920
Well, a lot.

88
00:04:55,129 --> 00:04:59,675
I mean, assuming he enjoys dinner, he might want a scoop of meemaw for dessert.

89
00:04:59,884 --> 00:05:01,260
Mom, the kids.

90
00:05:01,886 --> 00:05:03,471
I said "dessert."

91
00:05:03,679 --> 00:05:05,556
Did you know I was talking about sex?

92
00:05:06,098 --> 00:05:06,807
No.

93
00:05:07,016 --> 00:05:08,267
See?

94
00:05:10,394 --> 00:05:11,520
Why aren't you eating?

95
00:05:12,271 --> 00:05:13,898
I don't think I can.

96
00:05:14,106 --> 00:05:16,442
Sometimes the different foods touch each other, Sheldon.

97
00:05:16,650 --> 00:05:17,735
Not the end of the world.

98
00:05:17,943 --> 00:05:20,321
No, I'm afraid I'll choke again.

99
00:05:20,696 --> 00:05:23,032
Oh, honey, that's not gonna happen.

100
00:05:23,240 --> 00:05:24,450
Go ahead, eat your dinner.

101
00:05:24,658 --> 00:05:26,118
What's all this about choking?

102
00:05:26,368 --> 00:05:27,912
Sheldon almost died this morning.

103
00:05:28,120 --> 00:05:29,455
What?! Oh, yeah.

104
00:05:29,663 --> 00:05:31,999
Dad was shaking him upside down like a ketchup bottle.

105
00:05:32,208 --> 00:05:33,501
It was great.

106
00:05:33,751 --> 00:05:35,294
Well, you got to eat something, baby.

107
00:05:35,503 --> 00:05:36,629
Just have a few bites.

108
00:05:36,837 --> 00:05:38,756
I can't. It's too risky.

109
00:05:38,964 --> 00:05:40,216
Dibs on the tater tots.

110
00:05:40,549 --> 00:05:44,595
All right, I'll wrap that up for you, and if you change your mind, you can have some later.

111
00:05:44,804 --> 00:05:46,013
At least drink your milk.

112
00:05:46,847 --> 00:05:47,847
Sure.

113
00:05:48,015 --> 00:05:55,606
My mother and father saw that I'd been traumatized and were patient and understanding about my fear of eating solid foods.

114
00:05:56,023 --> 00:05:57,691
This lasted one more day.

115
00:05:57,900 --> 00:06:03,042
Sheldon Lee Cooper, if you don't finish that pork chop, I swear I will chew it up and spit it in your mouth like a mama bird.

116
00:06:03,197 --> 00:06:04,197
Do it, mom. Do it.

117
00:06:04,323 --> 00:06:06,635
Mary, can I speak to you for a second? No. Enough is enough.

118
00:06:06,659 --> 00:06:08,285
The boy has got to eat.

119
00:06:08,494 --> 00:06:09,662
Just give me a minute.

120
00:06:12,665 --> 00:06:14,083
Start eating, mister.

121
00:06:17,753 --> 00:06:19,088
You don't mind, right?

122
00:06:22,550 --> 00:06:27,137
How about I cut that pork chop off the bone and throw it in the blender with some gravy?

123
00:06:28,764 --> 00:06:31,433
If it fits through a bendy straw, I'll drink it.

124
00:06:31,642 --> 00:06:32,768
Coming up.

125
00:06:34,478 --> 00:06:36,105
Why does he get a pork shake?

126
00:06:36,313 --> 00:06:37,523
This is all my fault.

127
00:06:37,731 --> 00:06:41,193
I rushed him through breakfast the other day, and now look... He is wasting away.

128
00:06:41,402 --> 00:06:43,320
Oh, come on. You can't blame yourself.

129
00:06:43,529 --> 00:06:47,700
This isn't gonna last. Remember the time a bee got in the house and he slept under a net for a couple months?

130
00:06:47,908 --> 00:06:49,159
This is different.

131
00:06:49,368 --> 00:06:51,328
How? First of all, you let that bee in.

132
00:06:51,537 --> 00:06:52,538
The bee was your fault.

133
00:06:52,746 --> 00:06:55,457
Okay. Secondly, this is about his health.

134
00:06:55,666 --> 00:06:56,750
He is a fragile little boy.

135
00:06:56,959 --> 00:06:58,127
He can't go on like this.

136
00:06:58,335 --> 00:06:59,420
I'm not arguing with you.

137
00:06:59,628 --> 00:07:02,006
Let's just, you know, give him a little more time.

138
00:07:07,344 --> 00:07:08,554
Now what?

139
00:07:08,846 --> 00:07:10,598
Sounds like your mom's making margaritas.

140
00:07:14,018 --> 00:07:15,018
What's he drinking?

141
00:07:15,185 --> 00:07:16,896
Pork chop and gravy.

142
00:07:17,563 --> 00:07:19,207
I snuck some broccoli in there, too.

143
00:07:19,231 --> 00:07:20,941
Shh...

144
00:07:41,003 --> 00:07:42,755
I'm real glad you've been getting out again.

145
00:07:42,963 --> 00:07:44,465
Me, too.

146
00:07:44,673 --> 00:07:47,009
We had a nice dinner, took a walk.

147
00:07:47,384 --> 00:07:49,053
He's a divorcé, you know.

148
00:07:49,261 --> 00:07:50,262
I didn't.

149
00:07:50,471 --> 00:07:52,097
It's not a big deal for those people.

150
00:07:52,306 --> 00:07:55,267
If they're not a lawyer, they're related to one.

151
00:07:56,310 --> 00:08:01,649
George sr.: You're actually cutting the crust off before you blend it? I left it on his tuna sandwich yesterday, he said he could tell.

152
00:08:01,857 --> 00:08:03,108
Only drank half of it.

153
00:08:04,026 --> 00:08:05,786
Mary, you think this has gone on long enough?

154
00:08:05,986 --> 00:08:06,779
No trouble.

155
00:08:06,987 --> 00:08:09,114
But it'd be better if I had two blenders.

156
00:08:09,323 --> 00:08:10,803
Be better if you stopped coddling him.

157
00:08:10,908 --> 00:08:12,409
Well, I don't know what else to do.

158
00:08:12,618 --> 00:08:16,205
I suggested that we take him to some kind of professional, but you said he'd outgrow it.

159
00:08:16,413 --> 00:08:20,709
Well, if you're looking for a psychiatrist, I bet my new fella's related to one.

160
00:08:23,545 --> 00:08:24,545
What do you think?

161
00:08:27,174 --> 00:08:28,174
Not sure.

162
00:08:28,968 --> 00:08:31,716
If we take him to a shrink, it feels like we're admitting something's wrong with him.

163
00:08:31,887 --> 00:08:34,390
He hasn't had solid food in five weeks.

164
00:08:35,140 --> 00:08:39,853
Well, if there is something wrong with him, it ain't constipation.

165
00:08:46,068 --> 00:08:46,694
Okay.

166
00:08:46,902 --> 00:08:48,529
Let's see if we can find somebody.

167
00:08:48,737 --> 00:08:49,780
Thank you.

168
00:08:50,990 --> 00:08:51,990
Hmm.

169
00:08:57,621 --> 00:08:59,123
Needs a few more cheetos.

170
00:09:00,290 --> 00:09:04,837
My mother managed to locate a family psychiatrist within our price range.

171
00:09:05,212 --> 00:09:09,383
No one seemed to be bothered that he had a coupon in the pennysaver.

172
00:09:09,925 --> 00:09:12,678
Is this man gonna make me eat solid foods?

173
00:09:12,886 --> 00:09:14,763
No, he just wants to talk to you about it.

174
00:09:14,972 --> 00:09:20,936
Did you tell him I have a lot to accomplish in my life and cannot afford to be killed by an unchewed sausage?

175
00:09:21,770 --> 00:09:23,564
Not in those exact words.

176
00:09:27,526 --> 00:09:28,861
Did you bring the coupon?

177
00:09:30,112 --> 00:09:31,321
Hi. Welcome.

178
00:09:31,530 --> 00:09:32,781
You must be the Cooper family.

179
00:09:32,990 --> 00:09:33,741
We are.

180
00:09:33,949 --> 00:09:35,367
Right on, right on.

181
00:09:35,784 --> 00:09:37,077
This must be Sheldon.

182
00:09:37,411 --> 00:09:38,412
Put 'er there.

183
00:09:39,371 --> 00:09:40,581
No, thank you.

184
00:09:41,498 --> 00:09:42,708
Right on, right on.

185
00:09:42,916 --> 00:09:49,798
Okay, well, here's how I'd like to start. Mom and dad, why don't you come talk to me first, and then, Sheldon, you and I have a little rap session.

186
00:09:50,507 --> 00:09:51,507
Folks?

187
00:09:56,513 --> 00:09:57,973
Right on, right on.

188
00:09:58,640 --> 00:10:00,642
Feel free to read any of my comics.

189
00:10:04,605 --> 00:10:06,690
And you guys feel free to sit on my beanbag!

190
00:10:12,029 --> 00:10:14,656
40 cents? Outrageous.

191
00:10:14,865 --> 00:10:19,453
As fate would have it, the comic ipicked up was called X-Men.

192
00:10:19,661 --> 00:10:26,001
Young mutants with incredible powers who were feared and misunderstood by the entire world.

193
00:10:26,210 --> 00:10:28,462
Hey, it's about me.

194
00:10:29,755 --> 00:10:33,634
Now, the thing you have to understand is Shelly is incredibly bright.

195
00:10:33,842 --> 00:10:39,264
I mean, his IQ is right up there with Albert Einstein and that English wheelchair fella.

196
00:10:39,515 --> 00:10:44,353
Cool. He's nine years old and he's in high school, so you can't judge him like other kids.

197
00:10:44,812 --> 00:10:50,192
I hear ya. George, tell him about how he's always going on and on about subatomic particles and such.

198
00:10:50,692 --> 00:10:52,277
Oh, he'll get to gabbin' about it.

199
00:10:52,486 --> 00:10:55,531
We're accustomed to his quirks and oddities.

200
00:10:55,739 --> 00:11:01,537
But this whole "being afraid of solid foods“ thing, it's got us thrown.

201
00:11:02,287 --> 00:11:05,743
Okay. Let me just start by saying you came to the right place.

202
00:11:06,375 --> 00:11:08,627
Sheldon and I happen to have a lot in common.

203
00:11:08,836 --> 00:11:10,671
I, too, was a special little boy.

204
00:11:10,879 --> 00:11:13,465
In the fourth grade, I was reading at a seventh grade level.

205
00:11:14,049 --> 00:11:16,092
And I don't have to tell you where I was by the seventh grade.

206
00:11:19,179 --> 00:11:20,389
Tenth.

207
00:11:24,768 --> 00:11:26,770
I'm gonna give the beanbag a whirl.

208
00:11:30,399 --> 00:11:31,483
What are y'all watching?

209
00:11:31,692 --> 00:11:32,776
Little rascals.

210
00:11:35,487 --> 00:11:37,531
You know most of those kids are dead, right?

211
00:11:37,739 --> 00:11:38,739
What?

212
00:11:38,866 --> 00:11:41,827
Alfalfa, stymie, buckwheat.

213
00:11:42,035 --> 00:11:43,328
Even petey the dog?

214
00:11:43,537 --> 00:11:45,497
Oh, especially petey the dog.

215
00:11:45,706 --> 00:11:47,207
Thanks for making it depressing.

216
00:11:47,416 --> 00:11:48,584
You're very welcome.

217
00:11:48,792 --> 00:11:50,210
Y'all want to go get some ice cream?

218
00:11:50,419 --> 00:11:51,211
What about dinner?

219
00:11:51,420 --> 00:11:53,172
What about it? Come on.

220
00:11:54,506 --> 00:11:55,674
Is alf dead?

221
00:11:55,966 --> 00:11:58,010
No, but it's just a matter of time.

222
00:11:58,969 --> 00:12:01,129
You'd be surprised how many children have food phobias.

223
00:12:01,221 --> 00:12:06,810
Is that so? Oh, yeah. When I was Sheldon's age, I was afraid of that weird skin on top of puddin'. Really?

224
00:12:07,019 --> 00:12:08,913
Me, too. You know, you put a little saran wrap on...

225
00:12:08,937 --> 00:12:09,980
George.

226
00:12:13,025 --> 00:12:14,026
Sheldon?

227
00:12:14,860 --> 00:12:15,860
Where the hell did he go?

228
00:12:16,695 --> 00:12:17,695
Sheldon?

229
00:12:18,155 --> 00:12:19,198
Couldn't have gone far.

230
00:12:19,698 --> 00:12:20,698
Why not?

231
00:12:21,241 --> 00:12:22,242
Right on, right on.

232
00:12:25,245 --> 00:12:26,389
I checked the bathroom, the stairs.

233
00:12:26,413 --> 00:12:27,497
He's not in the building.

234
00:12:27,706 --> 00:12:29,266
I'm worried. No one's answering at home.

235
00:12:29,416 --> 00:12:30,626
Stay calm. We'll find him.

236
00:12:31,126 --> 00:12:32,753
You know, when I was a kid...

237
00:12:32,961 --> 00:12:34,213
Shut up.

238
00:12:34,922 --> 00:12:35,922
Well...

239
00:12:40,302 --> 00:12:46,600
It wasn't typical for me to wander out into the urban jungle on my own, but I was on a mission.

240
00:12:46,808 --> 00:12:52,272
I had gotten my first taste of serialized superheroes, and I needed more.

241
00:13:00,197 --> 00:13:03,242
Excuse me, do you have X-Men #137?

242
00:13:03,450 --> 00:13:06,245
I'd like to know what happens next, please.

243
00:13:06,745 --> 00:13:08,705
Back bin in the corner.

244
00:13:10,290 --> 00:13:11,625
What are you doing here?

245
00:13:11,833 --> 00:13:13,168
Oh, hello, tam.

246
00:13:13,377 --> 00:13:15,462
I thought you said comic books are for children.

247
00:13:15,754 --> 00:13:17,673
I'm a complicated young man.

248
00:13:21,218 --> 00:13:22,487
I think we should call the police.

249
00:13:22,511 --> 00:13:24,596
We don't need the police. We'll find him.

250
00:13:24,805 --> 00:13:26,098
This is all my fault.

251
00:13:26,306 --> 00:13:28,433
He ran away because we took him to a therapist.

252
00:13:29,142 --> 00:13:30,644
That's $15 we ain't getting back.

253
00:13:30,852 --> 00:13:32,688
That's what you're worried about?

254
00:13:33,021 --> 00:13:34,982
No. Course not.

255
00:13:37,526 --> 00:13:39,069
And Sheldon's fine.

256
00:13:39,861 --> 00:13:41,154
You know what he's like.

257
00:13:41,446 --> 00:13:44,366
If someone took him, I'm sure they'll bring him right back.

258
00:13:55,794 --> 00:13:59,381
I'm enjoying this, but I'm having trouble with the onomatopoeia.

259
00:14:01,383 --> 00:14:02,050
The what?

260
00:14:02,259 --> 00:14:03,635
The onomatopoeia.

261
00:14:03,844 --> 00:14:05,721
Words that imitate sounds.

262
00:14:05,929 --> 00:14:08,348
"Kthoom." "snikt."

263
00:14:08,682 --> 00:14:09,682
"Bamf."

264
00:14:09,766 --> 00:14:11,727
Writers shouldn't make up words.

265
00:14:12,311 --> 00:14:14,563
Somebody made up “onomatopoeia."

266
00:14:17,232 --> 00:14:19,901
You challenge me. I like that.

267
00:14:28,410 --> 00:14:30,090
Meemaw, are you gonna have any more babies?

268
00:14:30,245 --> 00:14:31,830
Good lord, no!

269
00:14:32,039 --> 00:14:33,665
She's too old to have any more babies.

270
00:14:33,874 --> 00:14:40,172
Don't have to have 'em. You live long enough, your hair and your teeth start falling out, you start wetting the bed, you get to be one.

271
00:14:40,380 --> 00:14:41,380
That's funny.

272
00:14:41,423 --> 00:14:43,133
We're gonna have to put a diaper on you.

273
00:14:43,342 --> 00:14:45,427
You won't be laughing when you're doing it.

274
00:14:45,761 --> 00:14:46,803
I might be.

275
00:14:50,307 --> 00:14:54,061
Hey, meemaw, next to Sheldon, who's smarter, me or Georgie?

276
00:14:54,269 --> 00:14:55,269
Me, of course.

277
00:14:55,520 --> 00:14:56,271
Why you?

278
00:14:56,480 --> 00:14:57,939
My head's bigger.

279
00:14:58,148 --> 00:14:59,608
It doesn't matter.

280
00:14:59,816 --> 00:15:01,860
Tonight is not about Sheldon.

281
00:15:02,569 --> 00:15:05,364
Yeah, right. Everything's always about Sheldon.

282
00:15:05,572 --> 00:15:08,617
Yeah. Most of the time, it's like we don't even exist.

283
00:15:09,701 --> 00:15:12,579
I guess Sheldon does get most of the attention.

284
00:15:12,788 --> 00:15:14,206
Maybe that's a good thing.

285
00:15:15,165 --> 00:15:16,165
How?

286
00:15:16,291 --> 00:15:19,795
Well, if it weren't for him, your parents would be on your ass all the time.

287
00:15:20,003 --> 00:15:22,923
Yeah. I guess I never thought about it that way before.

288
00:15:23,131 --> 00:15:29,513
You kidding me? Right now, they would be nagging you about cleaning your room and doing your homework.

289
00:15:29,721 --> 00:15:35,102
Instead, you're sitting in a parking lot, eating a blizzard for dinner.

290
00:15:35,310 --> 00:15:37,562
With cookie dough in it. Exactly.

291
00:15:37,771 --> 00:15:41,650
While Sheldon is stuck sitting in some boring shrink's office.

292
00:15:41,858 --> 00:15:44,277
Mom told me they were taking him for a haircut.

293
00:15:45,570 --> 00:15:47,572
Oh. Yeah.

294
00:15:47,781 --> 00:15:49,074
Right.

295
00:15:49,282 --> 00:15:52,953
Well, when he gets home, say his hair looks good.

296
00:16:08,552 --> 00:16:09,552
what?

297
00:16:10,595 --> 00:16:11,638
Nothing.

298
00:16:24,359 --> 00:16:25,359
You want one?

299
00:16:26,278 --> 00:16:27,278
No.

300
00:16:28,196 --> 00:16:29,196
Well...

301
00:16:29,322 --> 00:16:31,700
If you change your mind, help yourself.

302
00:16:34,286 --> 00:16:45,464
[Didn't have to read many comic books to understand that every superhero had a weakness, something they had to overcome through an extraordinary act of courage.

303
00:16:46,590 --> 00:16:49,384
For cyclops, it was the loss of Jean grey.

304
00:16:49,676 --> 00:16:51,761
For rogue, it was human touch.

305
00:16:51,970 --> 00:16:54,931
For me, it was food that required chewing.

306
00:16:55,140 --> 00:16:57,976
So if! Truly was a mutant, I would have to do the same.

307
00:17:11,948 --> 00:17:15,410
On this day, I would not be defeated.

308
00:17:17,829 --> 00:17:19,831
Because this was the day I became...

309
00:17:21,708 --> 00:17:23,126
The chewer.

310
00:17:24,544 --> 00:17:28,507
Just like that, I overcame my fear of choking.

311
00:17:28,715 --> 00:17:42,395
All that was left was my fear of dogs, birds, insects, germs, hugging, button fly pants, rivers, ponds, lakes, oceans, estuaries, corduroy, root vegetables, squeaky balloons, tinted windows,

312
00:17:42,604 --> 00:17:49,903
take a penny, leave a penny, fireworks, potbelly stoves, dust bunnies, that fuzz on peaches. Ugh.

313
00:17:53,281 --> 00:18:04,543
What I find interesting is how many supervillains are scientists: Doctor octopus, doctor doom, Lex Luthor, green goblin, the list goes on and on.

314
00:18:04,918 --> 00:18:05,918
So?

315
00:18:05,961 --> 00:18:08,964
So if the world doesn't respect me, I might change sides.

316
00:18:12,801 --> 00:18:14,010
Uh-oh.

317
00:18:14,886 --> 00:18:16,012
Good luck.

318
00:18:23,770 --> 00:18:32,112
I tried to explain to my parents that a mutant named cyclops, who shoots laser beams out of his eyes, helped me eat a licorice stick.

319
00:18:32,320 --> 00:18:34,155
Went right over their heads.
